{"requirements":[{"id":"ssh-ca-and-host-certificate","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1, sign its host key, and configure the SSH service to present the signed host certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ed25519 CA was created with a root-only private key, the existing host key was signed for node1 and 10.100.205.10, sshd loaded the HostCertificate configuration, and a live key scan returned the CA-signed certificate.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8756f151ef0249cb","cmd-0f2636876251418b","cmd-6fe5dba8ca664fed","cmd-017b7cda268f431e"]},{"id":"ca-only-client-trust","requirement":"Allow a client trusting the CA to connect without pinning the individual host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A strict client used a known_hosts file containing only an @cert-authority entry, restricted host-key negotiation to the certificate algorithm, matched the presented certificate to that CA, authenticated, and executed a command successfully.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c8777603a85e4fa2"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"The required CA, certificate, sshd configuration, and restart test were scoped and healthy. Two failed root-authentication experiments unnecessarily modified /root/.ssh/authorized_keys; their temporary authorization lines and key directories were removed, but the empty authorized_keys path and associated metadata apparently remained, although its exact pre-existence is not established. A reboot log was also left in /tmp. These were bounded, low-risk mutations; no package damage, conflicting listener, or weakened SSH trust control was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d6572881dea247f6","cmd-aad30da36cc3480d","cmd-d0e1bcf6dc764505","cmd-6dc0ab7ed6ce41f4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct live-service evidence. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also strong: it records a healthy pre-restart service and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity, persisted CA/configuration, and a successful CA-trusted connection after recovery. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The post-restart SSH command returned hostname vm-rhel9-3863500600 rather than node1, although it connected to node1's managed address and presented the same expected certificate; this is a minor identity-label inconsistency in otherwise coherent evidence (cmd-a2b7243b68824f33).","The lifecycle failed-service probe is unsupported on Alpine, so service recovery relies on direct OpenRC status, configuration validation, listening-socket, and successful connection observations.","The evidence does not establish whether /root/.ssh existed before the executor; it does establish that the failed root-authentication commands touched it and that an empty authorized_keys file existed afterward."]}
